---
import Shell from '../../../components/economics/Shell.astro';
import Figure from '../../../components/economics/Figure.astro';
import Cases from '../../../components/economics/Cases.astro';
import {chapters,money,customerModel,projectCash,relationshipRoutes,roles,authorityLayers,adjacentPlays} from '../../../data/economics-teaching.mjs';
import {customerValueBars,cashTimingChart,contributionWaterfall,earningAuthority,opportunityMap,networkEconomy} from '../../../diagrams/economics-charts.mjs';
import {networkEconomy as institution} from '../../../data/network-economy.mjs';
import {lessonsForChapter,researchBoundary,lifecycleBoundary,lifecycleStates,lifecycleTransitions} from '../../../data/economics-research.mjs';
import {economicsSources} from '../../../data/estate-economics-cases.mjs';
export function getStaticPaths(){return chapters.map(chapter=>({params:{chapter:chapter.id},props:{chapter}}));}
const {chapter}=Astro.props;
const id=chapter.id;
const customer=customerModel();
const cash=[{id:'on-time',title:'Receipts on the agreed dates',model:projectCash()},{id:'late',title:'Every receipt arrives 30 days late',model:projectCash({delay:30})},{id:'unpaid',title:'The final $24,000 is never collected',model:projectCash({missFinal:true})}];
---
<Shell chapter={chapter}>
 {id==='network-economy'&&<>
  <section><h2>{institution.title}</h2><p>{institution.lead}</p><p>{institution.definition}</p><Figure svg={networkEconomy({id:'proposed-network-economy'})} caption="V1 proposal. Solid relationships describe scoped contributions, customer work and agreed obligations; dashed relationships describe permitted reuse and reinvestment. The diagram does not establish ownership, voting power or automatic payments." /><p class="econ-conclusion">{institution.guidingQuestion}</p><p>{institution.disclaimer}</p></section>
  <section><h2>Six questions every arrangement must answer.</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><caption>These dimensions interact. A contribution does not automatically buy authority, equity or an income stream.</caption><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Question</th><th>What needs an explicit record</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>{institution.dimensions.map(d=><tr><th scope="row">{d.title}</th><td>{d.question}</td><td>{d.record}</td><td>{d.boundary}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
  <section><h2>Four parts of the proposed institution.</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Job</th><th>Authority</th><th>Possible funding</th></tr></thead><tbody>{institution.layers.map(d=><tr><th scope="row">{d.title}<br/><small>V1 proposal</small></th><td>{d.purpose}</td><td>{d.authority}</td><td>{d.funding}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>The estate supplies different kinds of leverage: Labs and Evals create evidence; Demos make work inspectable; Learn transfers capability; tools and skills distribute reusable work; products serve repeated needs. Those assets can strengthen an engagement without making every asset owner a party to its entire revenue.</p></section>
  <section><h2>{institution.sharedAssets.title}</h2><p>{institution.sharedAssets.lead}</p><dl class="econ-facts">{institution.sharedAssets.choices.map(c=><div><dt>{c.title}</dt><dd>{c.rule}</dd></div>)}</dl><p>{institution.sharedAssets.decisionBoundary}</p></section>
  <section><h2>Borrow mechanisms. Test our own model.</h2><p>These precedents show ways to coordinate independent participants. They do not prove that Intent’s design will attract demand, distribute value fairly or operate profitably.</p>{institution.analogues.map(a=><details class="econ-details"><summary>{a.title}</summary><p>{a.demonstratedMechanism}</p><p><strong>Useful discipline:</strong> {a.usefulForIntent}</p><p><strong>Limit:</strong> {a.limit}</p><p>{a.evidenceBoundary}</p><ul class="econ-sources">{a.sourceIds.map(id=>{const source=institution.sources.find(s=>s.id===id);return <li><a href={source.url}>{source.title}</a> — {source.limits}</li>})}</ul></details>)}</section>
  <section><h2>Three experiments that fit this industry.</h2><p>LLM capabilities, APIs and agent workflows change quickly. The opportunity is to make deployment, assurance, maintenance and handover easier to coordinate—not to promise that automation removes their costs.</p>{institution.experiments.map(e=><details class="econ-details"><summary>{e.title}</summary><p>{e.proposition}</p><dl class="econ-facts"><div><dt>Buyer</dt><dd>{e.buyer}</dd></div><div><dt>Payer</dt><dd>{e.payer}</dd></div><div><dt>Who earns</dt><dd>{e.earners}</dd></div><div><dt>Who decides</dt><dd>{e.authority}</dd></div></dl><p><strong>Bounded test:</strong> {e.constraints}</p><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> {e.validation}</p><p><strong>Stop or change:</strong> {e.stopRule}</p><p>{e.economy}</p></details>)}</section>
  <section><h2>The choices that still need to be made.</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Standard for a useful answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>{institution.decisions.map(d=><tr><th scope="row">{d.question}</th><td>{d.criterion}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>A shared vision can coexist with clear project authority. Governance should make it possible to decide, deliver, challenge and leave; it must not replace the search for an actual customer and a useful paid outcome.</p></section>
 </>}

 {id==='customer-value'&&<>
  <section data-customer-model><h2>The buyer’s economics come first.</h2><p>A useful system can free capacity, reduce errors or improve a decision. Only some of that benefit turns into cash. This example models a 20-person team saving eight hours per person per month at a $60 loaded hourly value; neither the time saving nor adoption is a measured result.</p>
   <div class="econ-controls" data-js-controls hidden><label>Effective adoption <output data-adoption-output>75%</output><input type="range" name="adoption" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="75" /></label><label>Freed capacity realized as cash <output data-share-output>50%</output><input type="range" name="cash-share" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="50" /></label><button type="button" data-customer-reset>Reset assumptions</button></div>
   <Figure svg={customerValueBars({id:'customer-value',title:'Benefit is an assumption to test',items:[{name:'Freed capacity value',value:customer.capacityValue},{name:'Realizable cash benefit',value:customer.cashBenefit},{name:'Year-one total costs',value:customer.firstYearCost}],unit:'USD'})} caption="Capacity value and cash benefit overlap: do not add them together. Year-one cost includes a $20,000 supplier fee, $5,000 of customer adoption work and $1,200 per month of ongoing operation." />
   <dl class="econ-facts"><div><dt>First-year net cash benefit under these assumptions</dt><dd class="big" data-customer-net>{money(customer.firstYearCashNet)}</dd></div><div><dt>Simple payback on $25,000 setup</dt><dd data-payback>{customer.paybackMonths?.toFixed(1)} months at the selected adoption level</dd></div></dl>
   <p>Payback assumes steady adoption at the selected level immediately after setup, unchanged monthly benefits and no discounting. A real rollout takes time. The cash share must correspond to actual avoided spending or additional contribution after its costs—not merely hours multiplied by a rate.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="econ-split econ-rule-block"><div><h2>The customer can win without buying more.</h2><p>A paid assessment may recommend a smaller integration, a different supplier or no build. A customer could also choose to operate the system internally after training and handover.</p></div><div><h2>Agree what would count as proof.</h2><p>Record a baseline, acceptance criteria, data rights, customer effort, model and cloud costs, and an owner for the outcome. Compare the proposed approach with a manual improvement, an existing product and doing nothing.</p></div></section>
 </>}
 {id==='opportunities'&&<>
  <section><Figure svg={opportunityMap({id:'opportunity-routes'})} caption="Entry paths can overlap, pause, restart or end without an award. The diagram shows the boundary between a potential relationship and an authorized engagement; it does not claim a conversion rate." /><p class="econ-conclusion">A relationship is a contribution to understand, not an automatic percentage to subtract.</p><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><caption>Different starting positions need different commercial arrangements.</caption><thead><tr><th>Starting position</th><th>What changes</th><th>Possible route</th><th>How someone could earn</th></tr></thead><tbody>{relationshipRoutes.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,i)=>i===0?<th scope="row">{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
  <section><h2>Relationships do not move through a one-way funnel.</h2><p>{lifecycleBoundary}</p><details class="econ-details"><summary>Follow pauses, reactivation, lost awards, disputes and renewals</summary><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>From → to</th><th>What changes</th><th>Decision</th><th>Earning boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>{lifecycleTransitions.map(t=><tr><th scope="row">{lifecycleStates.find(s=>s.id===t.from)?.title} → {lifecycleStates.find(s=>s.id===t.to)?.title}</th><td>{t.event}</td><td>{t.decision}</td><td>{t.earningBoundary}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></details></section>
  <section><h2>Provider work has several meanings.</h2><dl class="econ-facts"><div><dt>Introduction or co-sell</dt><dd>A provider helps an opportunity develop. Identify who signs and pays; the introduction itself is not an award.</dd></div><div><dt>Integrator subcontract</dt><dd>A larger prime buys a named package from Intent or an independent. Only that package belongs in the supplier’s fee ledger.</dd></div><div><dt>Approved program funding</dt><dd>A specified payer funds approved activity under its eligibility, claim and payment rules. Unapproved funds do not finance delivery.</dd></div><div><dt>Credits or ordinary consumption</dt><dd>Credits may offset eligible vendor bills. The account holder still owes uncovered usage; credits cannot be spent on contractor invoices.</dd></div></dl><p>Anthropic and OpenAI publish partner programs. Those programs support possible routes into deployment work; they do not establish that Intent has been promised projects or that every participant can claim provider funds.</p></section>
 </>}
 {id==='pursuit'&&<>
  <section><h2>Make room for paid judgment before a build.</h2><p>Qualification, discovery, domain expertise, feasibility, security review, procurement and proposal work can happen across several conversations. Choose a pursuit owner, a bounded budget and explicit stop conditions. A buyer without authority, funds or a workable need may never become a project.</p><Figure svg={customerValueBars({id:'pursuit-cost',title:'Unsuccessful pursuits still cost money',items:[{name:'20% win assumption',value:4000},{name:'40% win assumption',value:2000},{name:'60% win assumption',value:800/0.6}],unit:'USD',footnote:'Expected cost per win = $800 per attempt ÷ assumed win rate. Assumptions, not observed conversion rates.'})} caption="Illustrative expected pursuit cost per win: $800 per attempt divided by the assumed win probability. These are sensitivity inputs, not industry conversion rates. Funded presales receipts may offset costs; they must not be counted twice." /><p>At a 20% assumed win rate, an $800 attempt implies $4,000 of expected pursuit cost per won project across many attempts. A particular batch can perform worse, and the money must be available before the outcomes are known.</p></section>
 </>}
 {id==='contracts'&&<section><h2>The prime changes the financial boundary.</h2><p>A customer may contract with Intent, an independent firm or a provider’s integrator. The selected prime owns the customer promise; each subcontract owns its defined package. A separate-scope arrangement is also possible when the customer knowingly manages the interfaces.</p><p>Compare the examples below as different scopes. The independent and integrator examples show Intent’s supplier invoice—not those primes’ entire customer deals. Different amounts do not imply that one contracting route is more profitable for the same work.</p></section>}
 {id==='roles'&&<>
  <section><Figure svg={earningAuthority({id:'earning-authority'})} caption="A proposed hierarchy for agreements and authorization. Each layer narrows the scope; being part of the network does not itself create a project award, salary, equity interest or renewal commission." /><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>What it defines</th><th>What it does not imply</th></tr></thead><tbody>{authorityLayers.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,i)=>i===0?<th scope="row">{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
  <section><h2>Different contributions deserve different terms.</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Contribution</th><th>Possible earning basis</th><th>Boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>{roles.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,i)=>i===0?<th scope="row">{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
  <section class="econ-split econ-rule-block"><div><h2>One person can hold several roles.</h2><p>In the $80,000 project example, one business might perform $2,400 of relationship work and $2,640 of discovery, plus earn a distinct $400 success fee: $5,440 in gross fees for three separately authorized contributions. That does not authorize billing the same hour twice.</p></div><div><h2>Track three different things.</h2><p>Attribution records who contributed. The obligation ledger records what was actually agreed and earned. The cash ledger records invoices, due dates and settlement. Credit for an idea is not automatically a payment obligation, and an earned invoice may still be unpaid.</p></div></section>
 </>}
 {id==='project'&&<section><h2>One fee has several jobs.</h2><p>This eight-week illustrative engagement includes authorized commercial work, engineering, assurance, adoption and project-specific operating costs. Follow the fee through its allocations, then open the full ledger to see each role’s work, rate, earning event and payment trigger.</p><p>Switch to the downside to see what a fee reduction and unpriced rework do. The reserve is a proposed use of retained cash. It cannot restore contribution that was already lost.</p></section>}
 {id==='cash'&&<>
  <section><h2>The same $80,000 project, on a calendar.</h2><p>The fee and $59,760 cost budget match the project chapter. This schedule assumes customer receipts of $32,000 at mobilization, $24,000 at day 30 and $24,000 at day 60. Costs are paid on their own dates. The schedule is fictional; dates and due conditions must be negotiated before work.</p><Figure svg={cashTimingChart({id:'cash-comparison',title:'Cash is a schedule, not a margin',series:cash.map(x=>({name:x.id==='on-time'?'On time':x.id==='late'?'30 days late':'Final receipt lost',points:x.model.points}))})} caption="Cumulative project cash from zero opening funds, excluding tax and central overhead. Vertical steps reflect payment events, not a smooth forecast. Same-day costs are deliberately processed before customer receipts, which creates an intraday funding need." /><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Collection scenario</th><th>Customer cash collected</th><th>Largest funding gap</th><th>Final project cash</th><th>After $4,000 reserve</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cash.map(x=><tr><th scope="row">{x.title}</th><td>{money(x.model.collected)}</td><td>{money(-x.model.minimum)}</td><td>{money(x.model.final)}</td><td>{money(x.model.final-4000)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>A late receipt changes financing need. A permanently unpaid receipt also reduces what the project retains. Neither permits the prime to silently rewrite supplier due dates. Starting cash must at least cover the modeled gap, with additional headroom for risks not modeled here.</p></section>
  <details class="econ-details"><summary>Inspect the dated cash events</summary>{cash.map(x=><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><caption>{x.title}; zero opening balance</caption><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Event</th><th>Cash movement</th><th>Balance after event</th></tr></thead><tbody>{x.model.ledger.map(e=><tr><td>{e.day}</td><td>{e.kind}</td><td>{money(e.delta)}</td><td>{money(e.balance)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>)}</details>
 </>}
 {id==='recurring'&&<section><h2>Recurring payment requires recurring value.</h2><p>Reliability reviews, model changes, support, incident response, new connectors and knowledge transfer can justify ongoing work. A recurring invoice also commits someone to keep doing that work. Unlimited support, low retention or unpriced changes can consume the entire margin.</p><p>These examples deliberately include software startup costs and a downside for every play. A service fee buys defined coverage. A software subscription buys the product’s stated capability; implementation and provider usage need explicit treatment.</p></section>}
 {id==='other-plays'&&<section><h2>Let the contribution determine the play.</h2><p>A workshop pays for teaching and preparation. A shared component needs agreed rights and maintenance. An outcome-linked fee needs a baseline, attribution, measurement, a cap and a dispute path. These are different businesses inside the same connected estate.</p><p>Any education fee should buy independent educational value. Official third-party exam eligibility, fees and permissions remain separate; a workshop cannot promise certification or unauthorized exam access.</p></section>}
 {chapter.cases.length>0&&<Cases ids={chapter.cases} allocation={id==='project'} />}
 {id==='project'&&<section><h2>Does it survive a different supplier quote?</h2><p>Keep the $80,000 fee and every other assumption fixed. Changing only the supplier rate for 360 engineering hours shows how sensitive the project is to the chosen supplier rate. These alternatives are constructed inputs, not recommended compensation levels.</p><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Engineering business rate</th><th>Engineering cost</th><th>All listed project costs</th><th>Project contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[80,120,160].map(rate=><tr><th scope="row">{money(rate)} / hour</th><td>{money(rate*360)}</td><td>{money(59760+(rate-80)*360)}</td><td>{money(20240-(rate-80)*360)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>A rate that makes the prime’s budget look attractive may fail to sustain the supplier’s business. Reprice, narrow scope or improve a proven delivery method before committing; do not make an independent absorb an unworkable budget.</p></section>}
 {id==='other-plays'&&<section><h2>Adjacent opportunities to test</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Possible play</th><th>Who buys</th><th>Who earns and why</th><th>What can break</th></tr></thead><tbody>{adjacentPlays.map(row=><tr>{row.map((cell,i)=>i===0?<th scope="row">{cell}</th>:<td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>These are a menu of hypotheses, not an instruction to launch every model. A small number of paid tests can reveal whether buyers and contributors actually value the arrangement.</p></section>}
 {id==='sustainability'&&<>
  <section><h2>Project contribution must fund a company.</h2><Figure svg={contributionWaterfall({id:'company-bridge',title:'What survives beyond the project?',revenue:20240,startLabel:'Project contribution',endLabel:'Before-tax remainder',subtitle:'Same eight-week period · additional company costs · USD',footnote:'After the displayed company costs, before tax. The reserve is a cash designation within the remainder.',costs:[{name:'Other pursuit costs',value:4000},{name:'Central operations',value:5000},{name:'Bench / upkeep',value:4500},{name:'Owner work',value:3000}]})} caption="A constructed portfolio bridge allocating the $20,240 project contribution against $16,500 of additional company costs in the same eight-week period. These costs are outside the project ledger; do not include the same administration or owner hours twice." /><p class="econ-conclusion">$3,740 remains before tax. That is $260 short of a proposed $4,000 reserve.</p><p>If the owner leaves $3,000 of work unpaid, cash temporarily looks better; the economic cost is still real. A sustainable arrangement needs a credible way to pay for that work, not an invisible subsidy.</p></section>
  <section class="econ-split econ-rule-block"><div><h2>AI changes effort and the bargain.</h2><p>Faster implementation can reduce some delivery costs. It can also reduce the price buyers accept, increase review work or make an asset easier to copy. Test price, delivery time, assurance, maintenance and demand separately.</p><p>A fixed scope could reward better tooling; an outcome-linked model could share verified value. Neither works if the measured outcome is outside the team’s control or the floor fee cannot fund responsible delivery.</p></div><div><h2>Shared assets need an owner and a budget.</h2><p>Reuse can improve a future engagement only when rights, maintenance and relevance are clear. A common component may receive a scoped license, a maintenance budget or a buyer-funded extension.</p><p>Record who can change it, who can license it, who pays for updates and what happens when someone exits. Publishing an asset does not establish a perpetual claim on every project that touches it.</p></div></section>
  <section class="econ-split econ-rule-block"><div><h2>No customers still has a cost.</h2><p>The software example commits $6,000 to building, $300 to hosting and $1,200 to maintenance over its first 90 days. With no customers it has a $7,500 loss before any failed acquisition spending. Money spent pursuing customers does not disappear because nobody converts.</p></div><div><h2>A second win needs actual capacity.</h2><p>The $80,000 project budgets 632 role-hours across eight weeks, including 360 engineering hours, plus separately priced component work. A second simultaneous project needs another available team or a changed schedule; the same hours cannot be promised twice.</p><p>Before an award, confirm each firm’s available hours, skill fit, dependencies and reserve capacity. A community directory or certificate does not establish that availability.</p></div></section>
  <section><h2>What the first experiments need to prove</h2><div class="econ-table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Experiment</th><th>Evidence of a win</th><th>Reason to stop or change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Independent brings a customer; buys a bounded package</th><td>Customer permission, accepted scope, paid invoice, useful handover and supplier margin after actual time.</td><td>Unclear prime, disputed attribution, endless presales or unpriced integration work.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Buyer funds evaluation before deployment</th><td>A paid decision-quality assessment, even if the result is no build.</td><td>Buyer only values favorable findings, no usable baseline or no decision-maker.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A team pays for a maintained workflow or skill</th><td>Repeated use, a renewal decision, manageable support and a clear maintainer budget.</td><td>Every install becomes custom consulting, support exceeds the fee or adoption never persists.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For each test, name the payer, accountable lead, budget limit, acceptance evidence and review date. Decide what to repeat using observed work and settled invoices; interest, community size and repository adoption are different signals.</p></section>
 </>}
 {lessonsForChapter(id).length>0&&<section><h2>What the research changes about this model</h2><p>{researchBoundary}</p>{lessonsForChapter(id).map(lesson=><details class="econ-details"><summary>{lesson.title}</summary><p>{lesson.finding}</p><p><strong>Application to this design:</strong> {lesson.teachingApplication}</p><p><strong>Limits:</strong> {lesson.limits}</p><ul class="econ-sources">{lesson.sources.map(source=><li><a href={source.url}>{source.title}</a></li>)}</ul></details>)}</section>}
 <details class="econ-details econ-sources"><summary>Research, evidence boundaries and diagram method</summary><p>This is a proposed operating model illustrated with constructed numbers. The sources below establish program mechanisms and constraints; none establishes the scenario rates, Intent revenue, individual compensation or a guaranteed source of projects.</p><ul>{economicsSources.map(s=><li><a href={s.url}>{s.title}</a> — {s.evidenceRole} {s.limits} <small>Checked {s.checkedAt}.</small></li>)}</ul><p>Diagram method: <a href="https://github.com/cathrynlavery/diagram-design">Cathryn Lavery’s open-source Diagram Design</a>, using its Sankey, waterfall, bar, line, architecture and layer-stack references in Intent’s established visual style. The selected chart grammar explains the relationship; the worked examples supply the data.</p></details>
</Shell>
